From 0a4c2689073e0a137f88a242ad146864ef17c286 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 01:42:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/14] Build capacity stops from real loads, and refuse to
 invent the rest

dispatch/capacity.py has evaluated stop sequences and appointment windows
since it was written: stop ceilings, out-of-route miles, a window that
closes before it opens, a delivery whose appointment opens before its
pickup, and a forward walk that arrives, waits, serves and drives on.

None of it had ever run. scoring.assess_capacity() called evaluate()
without the stops= argument, so every one of those checks was unreachable
code with passing unit tests -- the most convincing kind of dead code there
is. KNOWN_LIMITATIONS section 4 named this as the next piece of the work.

dispatch/load_stops.py is the missing constructor, and most of it is about
refusing to hand the engine something it would be right to reject.

Never a naive timestamp. The engine treats one as BLOCKING and it is right
to: "06:00" is not an instant. But Dispatch's own pickup_datetime is
routinely naive, so passing those through raw would have turned a correct
refusal into a false alarm on nearly every load. Values go through
timestamps.normalize() first, which resolves a bare local time against the
configured operating timezone and records that it did so.

Never a zero-width window. A single recorded time is an appointment time,
not a window, so the closing end stays empty and the engine reports the
gap. Setting end = start would produce a "complete" window that demands
arrival to the second, and almost every load would read as infeasible.

Never a zero drive leg. scoring._requested_drive_hours() answers 0.0 for an
unknown distance, which is right for a physical-capacity request and wrong
here: it claims the truck arrives the instant it leaves, and the engine
would project arrivals from it. transit_hours() returns None instead. Worth
recording that no Dispatch load carries distance_miles today -- Load has no
such field and create_load() refuses the keyword -- so the forward walk is
off for every load now in the system and turns itself on the day a distance
is recorded.

Never an invented dwell. service_hours stays None, which is what keeps the
walk off. A dwell of zero would make every appointment look reachable.
DISPATCH_DEFAULT_DWELL_HOURS turns it on when the real figure is known.

Never an invented stop. A load records a pickup and a delivery; this builds
those two. Deriving intermediate stops would be route planning, and
CLAUDE.md 5.5 forbids a second scheduling system.

What it buys immediately, with no new data recorded: a delivery whose
appointment opens before its pickup, and a window that closes before it
opens, are both BLOCKING findings on a real load instead of unreachable
branches. Demonstrated by hand, and a sound load raises nothing blocking --
a check that fires on good data is noise, and noise is how a real finding
gets scrolled past.

